import { caseQuestions } from "./cases";

export const associateAssessment = [
  ...caseQuestions("Harbor trail reopening", "An accessibility coordinator prepares reopening notices for three trails in a shared Claude Project called Trail Notices. Signed operations bulletins govern hours, volunteer photos record physical conditions, and the ranger approves any change to opening dates. Bulletins sync from a Google Drive folder, a notice-formatting Skill applies the house layout, and the team reports service outcomes to a board.", [
    ["trail-audience", "1.1", "prompt", "The ranger asks for a notice for visitors who use wheelchairs or walking aids about the North Loop reopening. The coordinator's first prompt, 'Write an accessible update about North Loop,' returned a cheerful 300-word overview of all three trails with no surface conditions, no reopening hours and no alternative route. The signed bulletin and latest inspection are already in project knowledge. Which revision would most improve the first draft?", "c", "Generic first drafts usually trace to missing description, not model capability. A fit-for-purpose prompt names the audience, the authoritative source for each kind of fact, the action the reader must take and the output constraints. Roles, examples and model upgrades help only after those gaps are closed.",
      ["Keep the prompt and switch the chat to Opus at high effort so the model reasons more carefully about accessibility needs.", "A model or effort change cannot supply the audience, sources or alternate route the prompt never stated. The generic output is a specification gap, not a reasoning gap."],
      ["Add a role line saying Claude is an experienced accessibility advocate, and ask it to be more specific and detailed this time.", "A role sets perspective, but the reader, the authoritative sources and the required alternate route are still unstated, so the draft stays generic."],
      ["Name the readers, cite the bulletin for hours and inspection for surfaces, require an alternate route, and cap the notice at 120 words.", "This supplies exactly what Claude lacked: who reads it, which source governs each fact, the action visitors need and a length constraint."],
      ["Paste three past notices the ranger liked as examples and ask Claude to match their structure, tone and level of detail closely.", "Examples steer format and tone, but past notices describe other trails and do not tell Claude which readers or current sources this notice serves."]],
    ["trail-order", "1.2", "prompt", "Staff hold three signed bulletins, nine volunteer photos and an operations contact who must confirm what any surface change means for public access. The editor requires a source for every statement. The coordinator lists the steps: 1) Draft the visitor notice in the Project. 2) Extract each fact by trail segment with its source. 3) Ask operations to resolve unsupported access implications. 4) Run the notice-formatting Skill. 5) Send to the ranger. Which order should the team follow?", "a", "Decomposition only helps when each stage feeds checked material to the next. Extract sourced facts first, resolve gaps with the person who owns them, and only then draft and format. Any order that drafts before the facts are settled lets unsupported claims flow into every later stage.",
      ["2, 3, 1, 4, 5", "Facts are traced by segment, operations settles what the photos cannot support, and only then is the notice drafted, formatted and sent for approval."],
      ["1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "Drafting first means access wording is written before anyone knows which statements the bulletins and photos actually support."],
      ["2, 1, 3, 4, 5", "The draft is written before operations resolves unsupported implications, so the access wording may have to be rewritten after the fact check."],
      ["3, 2, 1, 4, 5", "Operations cannot resolve unsupported implications before the facts are extracted, because nobody yet knows which statements lack a source."]],
    ["trail-required", "2.1", "grounding", "The accessible-route draft from the Project fills every template heading and cites the current inspection. The inspection reports a 5% grade for the first 400 m only and notes loose gravel beyond the bridge, yet the draft calls the whole route 'gentle and firm.' The bulletin governs hours, not surfaces. The coordinator has time for one review pass before the ranger sees it. Which check would best catch a route a visitor cannot use?", "d", "Fitness for purpose is judged against the source and the reader's task, not the template. A segment-by-segment trace exposes claims that stretch a source beyond what it measured, while version checks, self-review and precedent all leave an overbroad description in place.",
      ["Confirm the map and inspection are current revisions, then check that every heading in the notice-formatting Skill output is filled in.", "Version and field presence were already satisfied; neither catches the overbroad slope claim or the gravel reported beyond the bridge."],
      ["Ask Claude in the same chat to reread the inspection and confirm that each route description is accurate before the ranger reviews it.", "Asking the model to vouch for its own draft is not independent verification, and the same reading error can simply be repeated."],
      ["Compare the draft with last season's approved route notice and keep the wording the accessibility reviewer accepted for this trail before.", "Last season's wording reflects older conditions, so matching it can preserve exactly the kind of surface claim the new inspection contradicts."],
      ["Trace each segment's grade, surface and closures to the inspection, and mark segments it does not cover as unverified for visitors.", "This tests every access claim against what the inspection actually measured and exposes the segments where visitors may need another route."]],
    ["trail-citation", "2.3", "grounding", "A draft notice says North Loop 'reopens to the public Thursday' and cites bulletin OPS-114. Opening OPS-114 shows it authorizes private maintenance vehicles to enter on Thursday and says nothing about public access. Research also returned a news article repeating 'Thursday reopening' without naming a source. The ranger has not signed an opening order. What evidence would support publishing the Thursday reopening claim?", "b", "A citation proves only that a reference was produced. The claim needs a source with authority over the same activity and audience. When a cited document covers a different activity, look for the record owned by the person who can make the decision, not for more repetitions of the claim.",
      ["A Research report finding two more independent news articles that repeat the Thursday reopening date, each with a working citation link.", "More articles repeating an unsourced claim add volume, not authority. None of them is the ranger's decision about public access."],
      ["A signed public opening order from the ranger for that segment, since the reopening claim depends on the ranger's decision.", "The ranger approves opening dates, so only a signed opening order can support a statement that the public may use the trail Thursday."],
      ["A volunteer photo taken Thursday morning showing open gates and maintenance vehicles at the North Loop trailhead, attached to the notice.", "Open gates for maintenance traffic show physical conditions, not permission for public access, which only the ranger can grant."],
      ["A Project instruction telling Claude to cite only signed bulletins, followed by regenerating the notice from the same knowledge files.", "OPS-114 is already a signed bulletin. The instruction changes nothing about whether any source authorizes public reopening."]],
    ["trail-escalation", "2.4", "grounding", "The ramp inspection dated 3 May says the east ramp is closed to wheelchairs; the published notice dated 4 May still lists it as accessible. Both documents are signed, by different staff. The notice-formatting Skill flagged the conflict, and the weekly send is due in 30 minutes. The ranger is the accountable approver for access claims. What should happen before the notice is released?", "a", "When two authoritative sources conflict on a safety-relevant claim, the person accountable for that claim decides, with both sources attached. Hold only the disputed line so the rest of the work can proceed. Dates, model judgment and after-the-fact logging do not resolve who has authority.",
      ["Hold the ramp line, send the ranger both documents with the conflict summarized, and release the rest once the ranger rules on it.", "The accountable approver resolves the safety conflict with the evidence, and holding only the disputed line keeps the remaining notice on schedule."],
      ["Publish with the notice's wording because its date is later, and add a sentence asking visitors to report any access problems they meet.", "A later date does not make the notice correct about the ramp, and visitor reports would surface the error only after someone is affected."],
      ["Ask Claude at max effort to weigh both documents and choose the more reliable one, then publish that wording with both citations.", "Deeper reasoning cannot settle which signed document reflects the ranger's decision; the conflict needs the accountable person, not a model's choice."],
      ["Publish the inspection wording for visitors, keep the notice wording for the board, and record the discrepancy in next week's operations log.", "Logging is detective, not preventive. Two audiences would receive contradictory access claims before anyone with authority resolved the conflict."]],
    ["trail-share", "3.1", "projects", "Four coordinators draft notices for different trails. All need the same signed bulletins and notice structure, but each trail's photos and draft history must stay separate so details from one trail do not surface in another. The bulletins change monthly in a Google Drive folder. The team is on a Team plan with Projects and the Drive connector enabled. Which setup fits?", "d", "Match each need to the capability built for it: shared reference material and standing rules belong in one Project, live documents arrive through a connector, and separate chats keep case histories apart. Duplicated copies, a shared thread or memory each fail one of those needs.",
      ["Create four separate Projects, each with its own uploaded copy of the bulletins, and ask each coordinator to refresh the copy monthly.", "Four snapshots must be replaced by hand each month, so one missed refresh leaves a coordinator drafting from an outdated bulletin."],
      ["Use one shared chat for all four coordinators and prefix each request with the trail name so Claude keeps the drafts apart.", "A single thread mixes every trail's photos and drafts in the same context, which is exactly the cross-trail carryover the team must avoid."],
      ["Store the bulletins and notice structure in each coordinator's account memory so every new chat recalls them without any Project setup.", "Memory is for durable context about the user, not reference documents, and it would not update when the Drive bulletins change."],
      ["Use one shared Project with Drive-synced bulletins in knowledge, the structure in Project instructions, and a separate chat per trail.", "Shared knowledge and instructions reach every chat, the synced files stay current, and separate chats keep each trail's history apart."]],
    ["trail-target", "4.1", "eval", "Reopening notices missed their deadline in three districts last quarter. Logs show drafting took under an hour per notice, but evidence packets sat a median of six days waiting for ranger sign-off because inspection photos or segment IDs were missing. A council member proposes a public chatbot on the trails website. The coordinator must choose one pilot measure. Which measure tests the actual bottleneck?", "b", "Start a use case from the observed constraint, not from the most visible tool. Here delay sits in incomplete packets waiting for an approver, so the measure should track packet completeness and time to a decision-ready packet while the ranger's authority stays in place.",
      ["Median minutes Claude takes to produce a first notice draft in the Project, compared with the current manual drafting time.", "Drafting already takes under an hour, so even a large improvement there leaves the six-day wait for complete evidence untouched."],
      ["Days from inspection to a complete, source-linked packet ready for the ranger, and the share of packets returned for missing evidence.", "This measures the stage where notices actually stall and shows whether better packet preparation shortens the wait for sign-off."],
      ["Weekly count of resident questions answered by the website chatbot, compared with phone and email enquiries before it launched.", "A chatbot changes how residents ask questions; it does nothing about incomplete packets waiting days for the ranger's approval."],
      ["Share of generated notices whose wording matches approved past notices, as scored by the notice-formatting Skill's built-in template checker.", "Template conformity measures wording, not the missing photos and segment IDs that hold packets in the approval queue."]],
    ["trail-news", "4.5", "eval", "A four-week pilot covered 40 notices. Preparation fell from 18 to 9 minutes per notice, but verification rose from 4 to 10 minutes because staff checked every cited segment. Reviewers corrected 3 notices with wrong closure dates. The director wants one sentence for the board's budget paper and asks whether to quote the nine-minute saving. Which statement belongs in the report?", "c", "Capability communication should be calibrated: report the whole-workflow effect, the observed error rate and the oversight that remains. Quoting one stage's saving overstates value, dismissing the pilot understates it, and extrapolating beyond the sample promises results nobody has measured.",
      ["Claude halves preparation time, saving nine minutes per notice; verification is a temporary cost that will fall as staff gain confidence.", "Nine minutes ignores the six extra minutes of checking, and nothing in the pilot shows verification will shrink while errors still occur."],
      ["Claude adds six minutes of verification per notice with no quality gain, so the pilot shows no measurable benefit for the trails team.", "This understates the result: total effort did fall, and review caught the errors, so the pilot produced a real if modest benefit."],
      ["Total effort fell from 22 to 19 minutes per notice over 40 notices; reviewers corrected 3 closure dates, so checking stays required.", "This reports the net change across both stages, the observed error rate and the oversight still needed, which is what the board must weigh."],
      ["Claude saves nine minutes per notice, which across 1,200 city notices a year frees about 180 staff hours for other work.", "The figure uses drafting time alone and extends a 40-notice trial to every city notice without evidence that other notices behave the same."]],
    ["trail-settings", "5.1", "projects", "The Trail Notices Project has an instruction to write dates as 'Thursday 14 May' and a knowledge file, bulletin-april.pdf. Operations has issued bulletin-may.pdf with changed hours. The coordinator wants every new chat to use the May hours and keep the date style. A colleague suggests pasting the May hours into the Project instructions so they are 'always on.' Which setup fits?", "b", "Project instructions hold durable behavior; project knowledge holds facts that change. Put each requirement in the layer that matches how often it changes, retire superseded files rather than stacking them, and confirm the result in a fresh chat on a case the change affects.",
      ["Paste the May hours into the Project instructions beside the date rule, and leave the April bulletin in project knowledge for reference.", "Hours in instructions must be edited by hand every month, and the April file left in knowledge can still be cited for the old hours."],
      ["Swap the April bulletin for the May one in knowledge, keep the date rule in instructions, and test a fresh chat on changed hours.", "The changing fact lives in knowledge, the stable style rule stays in instructions, and the test confirms a new chat picks up the May hours."],
      ["Keep both bulletins in knowledge and add an instruction telling Claude to prefer whichever document was uploaded most recently.", "Claude cannot reliably rank files by upload order, so leaving the superseded bulletin active invites drafts that quote April hours."],
      ["Continue from last month's approved chat so its date style carries over, and attach the May bulletin to the first message there.", "The old thread carries April hours in its history, and an attachment in one chat does not reach other chats in the Project."]],
    ["trail-scope", "5.2", "projects", "Volunteers will use a Google Drive connector so Claude can read the 'Trail Bulletins' folder. A sibling folder, 'Volunteer Contacts,' holds emergency phone numbers and must not be reachable. Connectors authenticate as each user, and the pilot account belongs to the volunteer role. An administrator offers to run the setup test from her own account to save time. Which test establishes the access boundary before the pilot?", "a", "Connector access follows the authenticated account, so access tests must use the role that will run the workflow and must check both allowed and denied retrieval. Prompts restrict what Claude says, not what it can reach, and output review only detects exposure after it happens.",
      ["Sign in as a volunteer-role test user, retrieve a known bulletin, then request a contact file by name and confirm nothing is returned.", "This tests intended and denied retrieval with the same permissions the pilot will use, so the boundary is proven before real use."],
      ["Sign in as the administrator, list the files the connector can see, and compare the bulletin titles against the approved folder inventory.", "The administrator's account reaches more than the volunteer role, so this test proves nothing about what volunteers will be able to retrieve."],
      ["Add a Project instruction telling Claude not to open the Volunteer Contacts folder, then check three notices for any phone numbers.", "An instruction shapes behavior but is not an access control; the connector could still retrieve the contacts if the account can reach them."],
      ["Generate a week of notices with the connector enabled and review each output for contact details before it goes to the ranger.", "Reviewing outputs detects exposure after retrieval has already happened, and a clean output does not show the folder is unreachable."]],
    ["trail-retire", "5.4", "projects", "The Trail Notices Project worked well for months. This week a draft quoted an old reopening date, and its citation linked to bulletin-march.pdf, which is still in project knowledge beside the current bulletin. Project instructions were last edited in January, and the notice-formatting Skill is unchanged. A colleague suggests rewriting the instructions to stress accuracy. What should the coordinator change and test first?", "c", "A change in output must come from a layer that changed. Here the citation points straight at a superseded knowledge file, while instructions and the Skill are unchanged. Fix the responsible layer, then rerun a case where the old and new sources would give different answers.",
      ["Rewrite the Project instructions to say 'use only the current signed bulletin' more prominently, leaving the knowledge files as they are.", "The instructions are unchanged since January and the March file remains citable, so stronger wording leaves the actual cause in place."],
      ["Delete the account memory topics about trail dates, then rerun the same chat with both bulletins still in project knowledge.", "The citation shows the date came from a knowledge file, not memory, and leaving both bulletins active keeps the conflict alive."],
      ["Remove the March bulletin from project knowledge, then rerun a case where the March and current opening dates differ.", "This removes the source the citation exposed and tests a case that only passes if the current bulletin is the one being used."],
      ["Switch the Project to Opus at high effort, since a more capable model should prefer the newer of two conflicting bulletins.", "A stronger model improves reasoning, not grounding; with the March file still in knowledge it can cite the old date just as fluently."]],
    ["trail-authority", "6.1", "safety", "The parks director proposes letting Claude decide which trails reopen each week from volunteer survey scores and photo notes, so the ranger reviews only disputed cases. Reopening is a public-safety decision the ranger is accountable for, and a wrong call cannot be undone once visitors are on a damaged trail. Staff time is the director's main concern. Which use of Claude fits?", "d", "Classify use cases by stakes, reversibility and accountability. Claude can prepare evidence and drafts for an irreversible safety decision, but the accountable person must decide before anything is acted on. Thresholds, agreement rules and confidence scores all move the decision to the model.",
      ["Let Claude approve reopenings when survey scores pass a threshold, with the ranger auditing a weekly sample of those approvals afterward.", "A later audit is detective control; visitors could already be on a damaged trail before the ranger sees the decision."],
      ["Let Claude reopen a trail automatically when two independent volunteer reports agree, and send only conflicting reports to the ranger.", "Agreement between reports is not the ranger's authority, and an automatic reopening is irreversible once visitors arrive."],
      ["Have Claude rate its confidence for each trail and send the ranger only the cases where its confidence falls below 80 percent.", "Self-reported confidence is not a safety control, and high-confidence errors would bypass the accountable approver entirely."],
      ["Have Claude assemble each trail's evidence packet with cited photos and inspections, and draft the notice after the ranger decides.", "Claude saves staff time on preparation while the irreversible public-safety decision stays with the ranger who is accountable for it."]],
    ["trail-identifiers", "6.2", "privacy", "The office may use Claude's code-executed analysis to summarize barrier trends by category and trail segment ID. The volunteer form export also holds reporter names, precise GPS coordinates and free-text comments that sometimes name people. Policy permits only barrier category and segment ID to leave the staff system. Which TWO preparation steps should the analyst take before uploading?", "be", "Data protection turns on order: decide what policy allows, transform the data locally, check the result, and only then upload. Controls that act after upload, such as restricted Projects, incognito chats or asking Claude to drop columns, leave the excluded data already disclosed.",
      ["Upload the full export to a Project restricted to the trails team and ask Claude to output only the category-by-segment chart.", "Restricting who sees results does not change what was uploaded; names, coordinates and comments would already have left the staff system."],
      ["Screen the local extract for names, raw coordinates and identifying comment text, and confirm none remain before anything is uploaded.", "Checking the actual file about to leave the staff system catches identifiers that slipped through, before any disclosure occurs."],
      ["Replace reporter names with pseudonyms but keep precise coordinates, so staff can merge near-duplicate barrier reports during the analysis.", "Policy excludes precise coordinates regardless of how names are handled, and exact locations can still identify the people who reported."],
      ["Upload the export in an incognito chat so nothing is saved to memory, then ask Claude to drop the restricted columns before analysis.", "Incognito controls retention and memory, not disclosure; the restricted columns reach Claude before it is asked to remove them."],
      ["Build the extract locally with only the approved barrier category and segment ID columns, leaving every other field out of the file.", "Minimizing at the source means only the two permitted fields ever reach the analysis, which still supports the trend summary."]],
    ["trail-feedback", "7.2", "eval", "Reviewers logged edits on 40 notices from the Project. Six notices used closure wording from a bulletin retired in March, which turned out to be quoted in the notice-formatting Skill's example file; two had minor tone issues. Project instructions and knowledge are current. The coordinator can make one change before next week's run. What should the next iteration prioritize?", "b", "Use feedback to find the most consequential common cause, then fix it at the layer that produced it and retest the affected cases. Repeated factual errors outrank isolated style edits, and a fix is only confirmed when the cases that failed now pass.",
      ["Add a warmer tone example to the Project instructions to address the two tone edits, leaving the Skill's example file unchanged.", "Two minor tone edits matter less than six notices with outdated closure wording, which would keep recurring from the Skill's example."],
      ["Update the Skill's example file to the current closure wording, then rerun the six affected notices and check them against the edits.", "This removes the shared cause of the most consequential errors and confirms the fix on the exact cases where it was observed."],
      ["Merge the eight reviewer edits into a longer style section in the Project instructions, so Claude sees each correction in each chat.", "The stale wording comes from the Skill, so more instruction text leaves the source in place and buries the standing rules."],
      ["Switch to a higher-capability model and rerun last week's notices, using the first new output as the comparison baseline.", "A different model still reads the same outdated example, and a single new output is not a baseline that shows improvement."]],
    ["trail-effort", "7.3", "eval", "Each of four coordinators pastes the same 300-word notice checklist into every new chat. An audit of 60 notices found 9 missing the required accessible-entrance field, all from chats where an older copy of the checklist was pasted. Each paste and fix takes about five minutes. The team has Skills enabled on its Team plan. What experiment best tests a reusable improvement?", "c", "Recurring manual setup is a candidate for configuration. Package a stable procedure once, version it, share it, and then measure whether omissions and minutes per run fall compared with the current practice. Long threads and personal memory keep the copy-and-drift problem in place.",
      ["Ask coordinators to keep every notice in one long shared chat so the checklist pasted at the top stays in context for each request.", "A long mixed thread blends notices from different trails, and an outdated pasted checklist would still apply to every later request."],
      ["Upgrade the plan for more usage and compare prompt length and response time before and after, keeping the pasted checklist.", "Usage capacity and response time do not address the stale pasted copies that caused the nine missing fields."],
      ["Package the checklist as a versioned Skill shared with the four coordinators, then compare missing fields and minutes across weeks.", "One maintained version replaces four pasted copies, and the comparison shows whether omissions and setup time actually fall."],
      ["Move the checklist into each coordinator's account memory and compare missing fields after two weeks against the previous two weeks of notices.", "Memory may recall a version imperfectly and cannot be updated centrally, so each coordinator could still work from a different copy."]],
  ]),
  ...caseQuestions("Mobile clinic outreach", "A program office schedules mobile clinic events and drafts outreach in a shared Claude Project. Clinical decisions stay with licensed staff. Public outreach separates service hours from eligibility advice. Drafts may use approved public notes only, the signed schedule sits in project knowledge, and the office publishes in English and Spanish.", [
    ["clinic-rewrite", "1.3", "prompt", "An approved clinic reminder was rewritten for a grade-5 reading level with the prompt 'Make this simpler and friendlier.' Reading ease improved, but 7 of 20 rewritten reminders dropped the line telling patients to confirm their appointment by text 24 hours ahead, which is required. The source reminder and the model are unchanged. What should the editor test next?", "c", "Diagnose the specific gap before iterating. A rewrite that loses required content usually lacks a stated constraint saying what must survive. Add that constraint and replay the same inputs so the result shows whether the fix worked, rather than changing models or abandoning the improvement.",
      ["Add two warm example reminders from another clinic to the prompt and compare reading ease scores on the next twenty rewrites.", "Examples from another clinic carry no confirmation step, and reading ease does not measure whether the required line survives."],
      ["Revert to the original wording, since simplification has proved risky for required content, and end the rewrite pilot.", "The defect is a missing constraint, not simplification itself, so abandoning the work discards a real gain that a targeted fix could keep."],
      ["Make the 24-hour text step a required line the rewrite must keep, then rerun the same twenty reminders and count omissions.", "This adds the absent constraint and tests it on the same inputs, so any drop in omissions can be credited to the change."],
      ["Switch the chat to a higher effort setting so Claude reasons more carefully, and rerun the same twenty reminders unchanged.", "More effort does not tell Claude the confirmation line is mandatory, so the same omission can recur with more elaborate wording."]],
    ["clinic-task", "1.4", "prompt", "The office asks Claude for two things in one chat: fresh ideas for reaching farmworkers at evening events, and a public alert announcing next week's clinic hours and locations. The first combined prompt produced an alert offering 'free transport,' an idea borrowed from the brainstorm that the office never approved. Hours must come from the signed schedule in project knowledge. Which prompt design fits both tasks?", "b", "Generative and factual tasks need different prompting strategies. Brainstorming benefits from openness within constraints, while a public alert must be bounded by its source and say what is unknown. Mixing them in one request lets invented ideas leak into factual output.",
      ["Use one creative brief for both outputs, and have Claude mark anything uncertain with a question mark so staff can check it later.", "Claude did not treat the transport offer as uncertain, so a marking rule would not have caught it; the tasks still share one loose brief."],
      ["Split them: a brainstorm within stated limits, and an alert drawn only from the signed schedule that lists unknowns, not guesses.", "Each task gets the strategy it needs, and the alert can no longer borrow unapproved ideas from the brainstorm."],
      ["Require every outreach idea to cite last week's approved notes, and generate the alert from the idea the team likes best.", "This constrains ideation where freedom helps and builds the factual alert from a proposal rather than from the signed schedule."],
      ["Keep one prompt but add a role line, 'You are a careful public health communicator,' so that Claude applies the same caution to both outputs.", "A role adds tone but does not separate the tasks or bind the alert to the schedule, so the leak can recur."]],
    ["clinic-sample", "2.2", "grounding", "A draft notice says 'Three in four clinic visitors prefer weekday hours.' Its source is a survey of 40 visitors at weekday clinics only, of whom 30 chose weekdays. No weekend clinic was surveyed, and the notice will go to all registered patients. The program manager wants to keep a figure in the notice. Which statement does the evidence support?", "a", "A claim can only be as broad as the population behind it. When a group was never sampled, name the group that was measured and state who is missing rather than generalizing, softening, or discarding real evidence. Disclosing the sample size does not fix a population mismatch.",
      ["Of 40 people surveyed at weekday clinics, 30 preferred weekdays; weekend visitors were not surveyed, so their view is unknown.", "This keeps the real figure, ties it to the group actually surveyed, and states the gap instead of implying an overall preference."],
      ["Three in four clinic visitors prefer weekday hours, based on a survey of 40 visitors, with the sample size shown in a footnote.", "The footnote discloses size but not the missing weekend group, so the claim still covers patients the survey never reached."],
      ["Most visitors, including those at weekend clinics, prefer weekdays, because the weekday respondents showed a strong 75 percent majority.", "Weekday respondents cannot speak for weekend visitors, who were never surveyed and may have the opposite preference."],
      ["The survey says nothing useful about preferences, so the notice should drop all figures and simply say that feedback is welcome.", "The survey is valid for weekday visitors; discarding it throws away supported evidence instead of stating its limits."]],
    ["clinic-versions", "2.5", "prompt", "A verified change summary says the Tuesday clinic moves to 2 p.m., triage now starts 30 minutes earlier, and patients must bring their medication list. Nurses need triage timing and staffing implications; patients need the start time and what to bring, in English and Spanish. A coordinator proposes sending the nurses' memo to everyone for consistency. How should the office prepare both versions?", "d", "Adapt detail and vocabulary to each audience while holding the operative facts constant. Start from one verified fact set, write each version for its reader's next action, and check that shared facts match across versions before anything is sent.",
      ["Send the full nurses' memo to patients as well, translated into Spanish, so both audiences read exactly the same verified wording.", "Patients would receive staffing and triage detail they cannot act on, and the start time and items to bring would be buried."],
      ["Ask Claude for two independent drafts written from scratch, one per audience, so each one reads naturally without inheriting the memo's structure.", "Separate drafts from scratch can drift on the operative facts, such as the new start time, with no shared source to reconcile them."],
      ["Write the patient notice first, then trim it into a nurses' memo that keeps the patient wording so the facts cannot drift.", "Trimming a patient notice drops the triage timing and staffing detail nurses need, so their version is incomplete."],
      ["Draft both from the verified summary: triage detail for nurses, start time and items for patients, then cross-check the shared facts.", "Each audience gets the detail it needs, and checking the shared facts confirms the time and requirements are identical in both."]],
    ["clinic-format", "2.6", "artifacts", "Staff revise a call script beside the chat every week. The scheduling system imports a JSON array of unique event codes, but the official event export repeats a code once per supported language, so 14 rows yield 9 events. Last week a list pasted from a chat reply imported 14 codes and created duplicate events. Which handoff fits both consumers?", "a", "Choose a format for each consumer. People who revise text need an editable Artifact; a system import needs structured data whose values are computed from the source and checked against a known count. Values typed or retyped from prose carry no such guarantee.",
      ["An editable Artifact for the script, and a JSON array that code execution deduplicates and checks against the nine events.", "Staff get an editable script, and the import gets computed, deduplicated values verified against the known count of nine events."],
      ["An editable Artifact for the script, plus a JSON array keeping one row per language so the scheduler can trace each code to its source.", "Keeping one row per language reproduces last week's duplicates, which the scheduler then imports as separate events."],
      ["A single Artifact containing the script with event codes in a separate column, which staff copy into the scheduler each week by hand.", "Hand copying from a script column gives the scheduler no validated array and repeats the retyping that caused duplicates."],
      ["A unique code array typed by Claude in the chat reply, plus a PDF of the script that callers annotate between weekly updates.", "A typed list is not computed from the export, and an annotated PDF is not the editable weekly script staff need."]],
    ["clinic-tier", "3.2", "models", "A trial ran 200 reminder labels and 30 eligibility-memo reconciliations through Haiku, Sonnet and Opus. All three met the 98% target on labels. On reconciliations, Opus matched nurse answers 29 of 30 times, Sonnet 24 and Haiku 19. Labels run hourly at high volume; reconciliations run weekly, and errors affect who is told they qualify. Which rollout does the evidence support?", "c", "Match tier to the task, not to the workload as a whole. Use the fastest, lowest-cost model where it meets the measured target, reserve a more capable model for complex, consequential work, and keep human review where residual errors still affect people.",
      ["Use Opus for both tasks so the office runs one setup, since the reconciliation results show the lower tiers are unreliable.", "Every tier met the label target, so Opus adds cost and wait time on high-volume work without any measured quality gain."],
      ["Use Haiku for both tasks and have it report its confidence on reconciliations, sending low-confidence memos to nurses.", "Haiku missed 11 of 30 reconciliations, and self-reported confidence is not a dependable signal of which answers are wrong."],
      ["Use Haiku for labels and Opus for reconciliations, keeping a nurse on reconciliations since 29 of 30 still leaves errors.", "Each task gets the cheapest tier that meets its measured need, and review stays where a remaining error affects eligibility."],
      ["Use Sonnet for both tasks as the balanced middle tier, then revisit the choice after a month of live reconciliation complaints.", "Sonnet's 24 of 30 is measurably worse on the consequential task, and waiting for complaints turns patients into the test."]],
    ["clinic-history", "3.4", "projects", "A 140-message planning chat contains verified July hours, abandoned brainstorming about June hours, and three corrections that contradict each other. Claude has started proposing June times again. The next operator will plan August in a new chat inside the same Project, and project knowledge does not yet hold the July decisions. What should the operator carry into the August chat?", "b", "When a thread has drifted, carry decisions forward, not debris. A concise verified summary saved where later chats can reach it gives the next session a clean starting point. Automatic summarization keeps a long chat running but does not remove contradictions or stale ideas.",
      ["The whole 140-message transcript pasted into the new chat, so each earlier correction remains available to Claude for reference.", "Pasting the transcript reimports the contradictory corrections and June brainstorming that are already causing stale proposals."],
      ["A short handoff of the settled July dates and open constraints, saved to project knowledge, then a fresh chat that starts from it.", "The verified decisions persist where every Project chat can use them, and the drifting history stays behind."],
      ["Nothing extra; automatic context management will summarize the old chat, so the operator can simply keep working in that thread.", "Automatic summarization keeps the conversation going with its content intact, including the conflicting corrections, so the drift continues."],
      ["The last assistant message from the old chat, including its proposed times, since it reflects the most recent planning state.", "The latest message is the one proposing June times again, so carrying it forward imports the exact error to avoid."]],
    ["clinic-effect", "4.2", "eval", "Patients who ask about wheelchair access at clinics wait a median of three days for a reply. Logs show Claude drafts each reply in about ten minutes, but the draft then sits in an accessibility reviewer's queue staffed two mornings a week. A manager wants to buy a faster model tier to speed replies. Which data would best reveal the improvement opportunity?", "d", "Improve a process by measuring each stage, including waiting time, before choosing an intervention. When drafting is a small share of total delay, faster generation cannot move the outcome. Stage-level data shows where the queue forms and which change could shorten it.",
      ["Mean tokens and drafting minutes per reply by model tier, to estimate how much a faster tier would shorten overall reply time.", "Drafting takes ten minutes of a three-day wait, so a faster tier can only shave a sliver off the delay patients experience."],
      ["Total replies drafted per volunteer per week, to identify which volunteers are slowest and target coaching where output is lowest.", "Volunteer throughput says nothing about the review queue, where replies spend almost all of their waiting time."],
      ["Drafting minutes on routine replies that pass review immediately, applied as the expected improvement for the accessibility queue.", "Routine replies skip the queue that causes the delay, so their timing cannot predict what would help accessibility requests."],
      ["Time spent in each stage by request type, including the queue before accessibility sign-off and how often replies come back.", "Stage times show the delay sits in the review queue and reveal whether returns or staffing drive it, which points to the fix."]],
    ["clinic-retain", "4.4", "eval", "A licensed nurse signs every change to public eligibility guidance before publication. The office wants Claude to speed up preparing those changes, which take a coordinator about 90 minutes each. Last quarter, 4 of 25 published changes needed later corrections. The office manager suggests routine-looking changes skip the nurse once Claude is in place. Which integration preserves the decision?", "b", "Integrate Claude into the preparation stage and keep the existing decision owner as a release gate. Logging the owner's corrections turns oversight into evidence about draft quality. Sampling, model agreement and template approval each remove the human decision from individual changes.",
      ["Publish routine-looking changes from Claude's draft and have the nurse audit a weekly sample, keeping her signature for complex ones.", "A weekly audit catches errors after patients have read them, and 'routine-looking' is a judgment the nurse is meant to make."],
      ["Draft from the signed guidance files in a Project, keep the nurse's signature on each publication, and log her corrections to track draft quality.", "Preparation gets faster while the licensed decision stays in place, and the correction log shows whether drafts are improving."],
      ["Publish a change automatically when Claude and a second model agree on the wording, and route disagreements to the nurse.", "Two models agreeing is not clinical authority, and shared errors would publish without the required signature."],
      ["Have the nurse approve the prompt template once, then let changes drafted from that template publish without a further signature.", "Approving a template is not approving each change; individual guidance would reach the public without the nurse's review."]],
    ["clinic-instruct", "5.3", "projects", "The outreach Project serves eight volunteers. Every reply must state current clinic hours from the schedule file, use plain language, and send eligibility questions to clinical staff rather than answer them. Hours change weekly; the eligibility rule never changes. One volunteer drafted Project instructions that include this week's hours: 'Clinics open at 8 a.m.' What belongs in the Project instructions?", "a", "Persistent instructions should carry stable behavior and point to where changing facts live. Writing this week's values into instructions creates a second, decaying copy of the facts. Anticipate edge cases, such as eligibility questions, with an explicit rule rather than leaving them to each request.",
      ["Cite hours from the schedule file in knowledge, write in plain language, and refer eligibility questions to clinical staff.", "Each standing behavior is captured once, and hours are read from the maintained schedule instead of being frozen into the rules."],
      ["Clinics open at 8 a.m. this week; write in plain language and refer eligibility questions to the clinical staff on duty.", "The hours will be wrong next week, so every chat would start from an outdated fact embedded in the standing rules."],
      ["The latest staff-approved reply in full, used as the default reference for wording, hours and eligibility guidance in every chat.", "An old reply freezes its hours and may contain case-specific wording, so it is a poor substitute for explicit rules."],
      ["Answer each caller in one confident sentence, and state the clinic's opening time from memory so that replies stay short.", "Memory is not the maintained schedule, and the rule says nothing about routing eligibility questions to clinical staff."]],
    ["clinic-approval", "6.3", "privacy", "A contractor's Gmail connector already reads incident summaries for a safety review the data owner approved last year. Clinic policy requires separate approval for each purpose and connector. An outreach coordinator now wants to use the same connector to draft public reassurance messages about recent incidents. The contractor offers to share access today. What should the coordinator do first?", "c", "Technical access is not authorization. When policy ties approval to purpose, a new use of the same data needs the data owner's sign-off before any connection, covering the purpose, the connector and the fields. Temporary access, prompt rules and later review all act too late.",
      ["Use the contractor's existing connector, since the data owner has already approved this source and the messages are low risk.", "The earlier approval covered a safety review, not public messaging, and policy requires separate approval for each purpose."],
      ["Connect briefly for a one-week pilot, then disconnect and request approval if the drafts prove useful enough to continue.", "Policy requires approval before access; a short pilot still exposes incident data for an unapproved purpose."],
      ["Ask the data owner to approve this purpose, the connector and the fields needed before any connection is made for outreach.", "This follows the policy as written: the specific new purpose and access are approved before any incident data is used."],
      ["Add a Project instruction telling Claude to follow the clinic's data policy, then connect and review each draft before sending.", "A prompt cannot grant approval, and reviewing drafts happens after the unapproved access has already taken place."]],
    ["clinic-fairness", "6.4", "eval", "An intake helper labels requests by urgency. In a 200-request audit, short requests translated from Spanish were marked low priority 38% of the time, against 12% for English requests with similar symptoms and dates. Staff use the label to order call-backs. The vendor says the label reflects 'clarity of need.' What test best investigates this?", "d", "Investigate suspected bias with matched cases that hold the facts constant and vary only language or style. That isolates whether wording, rather than need, drives outcomes. Removing the affected group, asking the model to justify itself, or changing settings without a control all leave the cause unknown.",
      ["Drop translated requests from the audit sample, since translation noise makes them unreliable for judging the helper's accuracy.", "Removing the affected group hides the disparity instead of testing it, and those patients still receive the biased labels."],
      ["Ask Claude to explain each low-priority label and accept labels whose explanations cite a specific symptom from the request.", "A plausible explanation is self-assessment; it does not show whether an English request with the same facts would be labelled higher."],
      ["Raise the effort level for translated requests only, and compare low-priority rates for one week against the previous week.", "Week-to-week comparison confounds the setting with changes in request mix, and it never tests matched facts."],
      ["Run requests with identical facts in short, long, English and Spanish versions, and have staff judge the right urgency for each one.", "Holding facts fixed isolates language and length as causes, and staff judgment provides the reference for correct urgency."]],
    ["clinic-debug", "7.1", "eval", "Since Monday, clinic notices omit the last-admission cutoff. On Monday the events feed renamed closing_time to last_admission; the prompt, Project instructions and model are unchanged. The coordinator lists steps: 1) Update the instructions to treat last_admission as the cutoff. 2) Replay an old-format and a new-format record through the unchanged prompt. 3) Confirm the omission occurs only with the new format. 4) Rerun the new-format record. 5) Resume sending. Which order isolates and fixes the cause?", "c", "Reproduce before you repair. Replaying matched records with everything else held constant confirms the suspected input change is the cause; only then change the configuration and rerun the failing case. Fixing first makes it impossible to tell whether the change mattered.",
      ["1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "Changing the instructions first means the replay no longer tests the unchanged setup, so the cause is never actually confirmed."],
      ["2, 1, 3, 4, 5", "The fix is applied before confirming the omission is specific to the new format, so step 3 tests the changed setup, not the cause."],
      ["2, 3, 1, 4, 5", "The replay and confirmation isolate the renamed field as the cause before the fix, and the rerun shows the fix works."],
      ["3, 2, 1, 4, 5", "The omission cannot be confirmed as format-specific before both records have been replayed through the unchanged prompt."]],
    ["clinic-verify", "2.3", "grounding", "The East clinic's approved holiday calendar lists an 11 a.m. opening on 4 July. A newer signed bulletin moves the West clinic's opening to noon and states that it applies to West only. The East draft cites that West bulletin and gives noon. Research also found a community board post saying 'all clinics open at noon.' What action should the editor take?", "a", "Verify that a cited source covers the same population, place and time as the claim. A newer date or an informal corroborating post does not extend a source beyond its stated scope. Correct the claim to the source that actually governs it.",
      ["Restore 11 a.m. for East and cite the approved East calendar, since the West bulletin's stated scope excludes East.", "This corrects both the time and its provenance, using the source that actually governs East's holiday hours."],
      ["Keep noon and cite both documents, noting that the West bulletin is the more recent signed source and the board post agrees.", "Recency does not override the bulletin's stated scope, and an unsourced community post adds no authority for East."],
      ["State that East opens between 11 a.m. and noon while staff reconcile the records, citing both the calendar and the bulletin.", "The records govern different clinics and do not conflict for East, so a range invents uncertainty that is not there."],
      ["Remove the opening time from the East alert until every clinic location has signed one coordinated holiday schedule.", "East already has an approved calendar, so withholding the time leaves patients without a fact the office can support."]],
    ["clinic-limits", "6.1", "safety", "A service administrator wants Claude to decide which patients get 40 limited home-visit slots by scoring their intake records, and to send the decisions directly. Anthropic's Usage Policy treats healthcare decisions about individuals as high-risk, requiring a qualified human who reviews and can change each recommendation, plus disclosure that AI was involved. Which use fits?", "d", "For high-risk uses under the Usage Policy, Claude may inform but not make decisions about people: a qualified human reviews each recommendation with authority to change it, and affected people are told AI was involved. Disclosure alone, tie-breaking or after-the-fact audits do not meet that standard.",
      ["Let Claude approve straightforward applications automatically and send low-confidence cases to a nurse, disclosing AI use in letters.", "Disclosure is required but not sufficient; most decisions would still be made without a qualified human reviewing each one."],
      ["Use Claude as the tie-breaker when two nurses rank applicants equally, and disclose in each decision letter that AI was involved.", "In tied cases the model, not a qualified person, would make the final healthcare decision about who receives a visit."],
      ["Have Claude assign the visit order by urgency score, with a nurse auditing a weekly sample of those assignments afterward.", "A retrospective sample is not meaningful review of each recommendation before it affects a patient's care."],
      ["Have Claude summarize each patient's file against the criteria with citations; a nurse decides each case, and patients are told AI assisted.", "A qualified human reviews and decides every case with evidence Claude prepared, and disclosure meets the policy's transparency requirement."]],
  ]),
  ...caseQuestions("Theatre group bookings", "A regional theatre manages public performances and closed rehearsals. Staff draft patron communications in a shared Claude Project whose knowledge holds the approved performance calendar and access policy. A restricted donor file sits on the same shared drive. Staff review and send all final communications, and the theatre compares a short pilot with its manual workflow.", [
    ["theatre-spec", "1.1", "prompt", "A patron emails asking for two wheelchair spaces at Saturday's sold-out matinee. The agent's prompt, 'Reply helpfully to this patron,' produced a warm reply promising 'we'll find you seats,' which policy forbids before the box office confirms availability. The approved availability sheet and the access policy are both in project knowledge. Which brief makes the response testable?", "b", "A testable brief names the source, the audience's need, the allowed actions and what to say when the answer is no. Vague quality words, self-checks and roles leave Claude to improvise, which is how unapproved promises appear. If you cannot check the output against the brief, it is underspecified.",
      ["Ask Claude to be warm, helpful and accurate, and to double-check the reply for any promises before it returns the final draft.", "Vague qualities give no criterion to test against, and a self-check does not tell Claude which promises policy forbids."],
      ["Reply from the availability sheet and access policy; offer listed alternatives only, and say plainly if none are open.", "The brief names the sources, the permitted offers and the no-availability case, so any draft can be checked against it."],
      ["Add a role line, 'You are an expert box office manager,' and ask Claude to use its judgment about what the theatre can offer.", "A role invites Claude to decide what to offer, which is how the unapproved promise appeared in the first place."],
      ["Paste the full season brochure into the chat and ask Claude to suggest any performance likely to have wheelchair spaces.", "The brochure lists performances, not availability, so 'likely' spaces are guesses the box office has not confirmed."]],
    ["theatre-stage", "1.2", "prompt", "Twenty confirmation emails must reconcile tentative holds with paid bookings before Friday. Last month a batch confirmed three unpaid holds. The steps are: 1) Export holds and payments from the box office system. 2) Match booking IDs with code execution and flag unmatched rows. 3) Draft confirmations for paid bookings and reminders for unpaid holds. 4) Box office staff resolve flagged rows. 5) Staff send the messages. Which order prevents accidental confirmations?", "d", "Place the human checkpoint where an early error would otherwise flow into every later step. Payment status must be settled, including the rows that could not be matched automatically, before any message is drafted; otherwise drafting forces a guess about status.",
      ["1, 3, 2, 4, 5", "Drafting before matching means every message assumes a payment status that has not yet been checked against the export."],
      ["1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "Flagged rows are drafted before staff resolve them, so their messages rest on a guessed payment status."],
      ["3, 1, 2, 4, 5", "Drafting before any data is exported repeats last month's failure, since no draft can reflect actual payments."],
      ["1, 2, 4, 3, 5", "Payment status is matched and every flagged row is resolved before drafting, so no confirmation can rest on an unpaid hold."]],
    ["theatre-minutes", "2.1", "grounding", "A confirmation for a patron who uses a wheelchair gives the correct date, seat and price, but omits the access policy's requirement to arrive 30 minutes early for the lift, which the patron asked about. Staff currently check confirmations by reading them for tone. The team wants a review step that catches this across 300 confirmations a month. Which review detects it reliably?", "a", "Evaluate completeness against the requirements and source, not against polish. A field list derived from the governing policy, applied whenever its trigger appears, turns 'does this feel complete' into a repeatable check that catches omissions rather than only errors in what is present.",
      ["Check each confirmation against a field list from the access policy, including arrival and lift details when access is requested.", "Required fields come from the governing policy and are checked whenever access applies, so the missing arrival time is caught."],
      ["Ask Claude in a second chat to rate whether each confirmation feels complete, and flag any scoring below four out of five.", "A model's impression of completeness is not checked against the policy, so a fluent reply missing the lift detail can score highly."],
      ["Check that each confirmation includes the date, seat, price and performance title, since patrons dispute those fields most often.", "This field list omits the access requirements, so it would pass the exact confirmation that failed this patron."],
      ["Read a random 10 percent of confirmations aloud each week to catch awkward phrasing and formatting errors before patrons see them.", "Reading for phrasing does not test content against policy, and a 10 percent sample misses most access confirmations."]],
    ["theatre-refund", "2.4", "grounding", "A patron asks for a full refund three days after the published 14-day cutoff, citing a hospital stay. The refund policy allows exceptions only by the duty manager. Claude's draft says 'we're happy to refund you in full.' In comparable past cases the manager approved 7 of 10 similar requests. The agent wants to answer today. What should the reply do?", "c", "When an output commits the organization to something only a named person may decide, the draft must route to that person rather than predict or pre-empt the decision. Past patterns can inform the decision maker but do not transfer authority to the agent or the model.",
      ["Deny the refund under the published 14-day policy, since the agent cannot grant exceptions, and close the ticket today.", "The policy provides an exception path; denying outright decides the case the duty manager is entitled to decide."],
      ["Send the full refund as drafted, because past approvals show the manager usually agrees and a refund can be reversed later.", "The agent lacks authority to grant the exception, and a 70 percent approval rate means some similar cases were refused."],
      ["Tell the patron the duty manager will decide, keep the refund pending, and send the manager a summary with the evidence.", "The patron gets a timely, honest reply, and the person with authority decides using the evidence Claude helped assemble."],
      ["Use the seven past approvals to estimate the manager's likely decision and tell the patron that an exception is probable.", "A prediction communicated to the patron sets an expectation the manager has not approved and may not meet."]],
    ["theatre-export", "2.6", "artifacts", "The duty manager wants to edit next month's 26-performance schedule beside the chat, and the ticketing system imports dates as a CSV with the columns code, date and start_time. Last month, dates retyped from a chat reply were imported with two days transposed. Code execution and Artifacts are enabled. Which handoff is most useful?", "c", "Pick the container for each consumer: an editable Artifact for people revising content, and a structured file computed from the source for machine import. Values generated by code from approved data can be checked row by row; prose and screenshots require retyping, which reintroduces errors.",
      ["A screenshot of the schedule for the manager, plus a screenshot of the date table that the box office retypes into ticketing.", "Screenshots cannot be edited or imported, so the box office would retype dates exactly as it did when they were transposed."],
      ["The full chat reply pasted into one spreadsheet cell, with a note asking the ticketing team to split the dates into columns.", "The ticketing team would still parse prose by hand, which is the step that transposed last month's dates."],
      ["An editable schedule Artifact, plus a CSV generated by code execution from the calendar file and spot-checked before import.", "The manager edits beside the chat, and the import receives computed values in the required columns, checked before upload."],
      ["A narrative memo of the schedule that the manager edits, with dates copied into the ticketing system by hand afterward.", "A memo is editable, but the hand copy repeats the retyping that caused last month's errors in the ticketing system."]],
    ["theatre-find", "3.1", "claudeResearch", "The theatre is preparing a lobby exhibit card listing when twelve local venues opened. It has no source list, and several online reviews round the dates or disagree by a year. The editor wants to keep revising the card's wording beside the conversation. Research, web search and Artifacts are enabled. Which TWO choices satisfy those needs?", "ad", "Combine capabilities by need: Research for multi-source discovery with citations, primary records for decisive facts, and an Artifact for an editable standalone deliverable. A Project stores material but does not find it, and a static export cannot be revised in place.",
      ["Use Research to follow leads across reviews and archives, then confirm each opening year against a primary record such as a founding notice.", "Research handles discovery across many sources, and the primary record settles years the reviews disagree about."],
      ["Use a Project to hold a verified link list, seeding it with the dates mentioned in yesterday's brainstorming chat about the exhibit.", "A Project can store verified links, but brainstormed dates from an old chat are unverified and the theatre has no list yet."],
      ["Use web search for each venue's earliest review and treat that review's publication year as the opening year unless sources disagree.", "A review can appear before or after a venue opens, so its date is not evidence of the opening year even without a conflict."],
      ["Build the card as an editable Artifact beside the conversation, so the editor can revise its wording while the research continues.", "An Artifact gives the editor a standalone, revisable card next to the ongoing conversation, which is what was asked for."],
      ["Export the card as a signed-off PDF, and generate a fresh replacement copy whenever the editor requests a wording change.", "A PDF suits distribution, but every edit would require regeneration instead of revising the card in place."]],
    ["theatre-economics", "3.3", "models", "Message labelling on Haiku costs 1¢ per message, but 60% of address-change messages need an 8¢ staff correction. Sonnet costs 4¢ per message and needs corrections on 5% of address changes. Ordinary labels meet the quality target on both. Address changes are 10% of 50,000 monthly messages. Which comparison should drive routing?", "d", "Compare cost per acceptable outcome, including the human correction work each option creates, and do it per task type. A low list price can be the expensive choice once rework is counted, while a higher price is wasted where the cheaper model already meets the target.",
      ["Per-message model price across the 50,000 monthly messages, choosing Haiku throughout because its listed price is a quarter of Sonnet's.", "List price ignores the correction cost: on address changes Haiku averages 5.8¢ with rework against Sonnet's 4.4¢."],
      ["Average response time across the mixed workload, choosing the setup that returns labels faster during the busiest hour.", "Speed is not the stated constraint, and a blended average hides where corrections, not latency, drive the cost."],
      ["Sonnet's cost on address changes alone, since that is the one slice where the cheaper setup produced a quality problem.", "Looking at one option on one slice gives no comparison, so it cannot show which setup is cheaper per correct label."],
      ["Cost per acceptable outcome, counting staff corrections, worked out for ordinary labels and for address changes under each setup.", "Counting rework per task type shows Haiku is cheaper for ordinary labels and Sonnet for address changes, so routing can split."]],
    ["theatre-gap", "4.1", "eval", "Patron complaints rose 40% this season. Of 45 logged complaints, 31 concern wrong seats in confirmations and none concern how quickly emails arrive. The sponsor proposes using Claude to draft marketing scripts faster. Confirmations are drafted from the seating export and approved by box office staff before sending. Which pilot target is best supported by the evidence?", "b", "Identify the use case from evidence of user harm, then set a target you can measure directly while keeping the controls that already exist. A target that is easy to improve but unconnected to the observed problem, such as speed or length, produces activity without benefit.",
      ["Increase marketing script drafts per shift by half, since faster drafting frees staff time that can be spent on complaints.", "The complaints are about seats, not scripts, so this measures a gain nobody asked for and leaves the harm untouched."],
      ["Reduce seat errors in confirmations on a labelled sample of past bookings, keeping box office approval before every send.", "The target addresses the cause of most complaints, is measurable on known cases and preserves the existing approval step."],
      ["Cut the time between booking and confirmation by sending each confirmation before the nightly seating export has finished loading.", "Speed is not the complaint, and drafting before seats load would create more of the wrong-seat errors patrons report."],
      ["Shorten every patron confirmation to under 80 words, since shorter messages leave fewer places for seat details to go wrong.", "Length does not cause wrong seats; a short confirmation can still copy the wrong seat from the export."]],
    ["theatre-compare", "4.3", "eval", "A revised confirmation prompt adds an instruction to copy seat identifiers exactly from the export. The team wants to know whether it reduced seat errors, which ran at 7 per 100 confirmations. Last week they also switched models and replaced the seating file format, and errors fell to 3 per 100. What comparison establishes whether the new prompt helped?", "c", "To credit a change, hold everything else constant and vary only that change on the same inputs. When several things changed at once, a before-and-after difference cannot be attributed. Replay matched cases and score them against fixed criteria.",
      ["Compare last week's 3 per 100 with the earlier 7 per 100 and credit the prompt, since it was the change aimed at seat errors.", "The model and file format changed at the same time, so the drop cannot be attributed to the prompt."],
      ["Compare this season's error rate with last season's sold-out run, using the new prompt, model and file format together.", "Different seasons and three simultaneous changes leave nothing isolated, so the cause of any difference stays unknown."],
      ["Replay the same 100 bookings with the old and new prompts on one model and file format, scoring seat accuracy and omissions.", "Only the prompt varies across matched bookings, so any change in seat accuracy can be credited to the new instruction."],
      ["Ask box office staff which drafts sound more professional and precise, and adopt the prompt whose drafts they prefer.", "Perceived professionalism is not seat accuracy, and staff impressions do not isolate the effect of the prompt change."]],
    ["theatre-owner", "4.4", "eval", "Sales owns booking changes and records approved seat moves in the ticketing system. Patron Services owns every outgoing reply. Both teams draft in the same Project. A reply promising a new seat may go out only after Sales records the move, but most replies involve no seat change and should not wait. Last week a reply promised a seat that Sales later refused. Which workflow preserves both authorities without holding unrelated replies?", "a", "When integrating Claude into an existing process, map each claim in the output to the person who owns it and gate only the claims that need that owner. Gates should sit before the final release, not at intake, and should not hold work that does not depend on them.",
      ["Draft in parallel; for seat changes, Sales must log the move before Patron Services gives final sign-off; other replies go out normally.", "The seat claim is checked against a recorded move, Patron Services keeps release authority, and unrelated replies are not delayed."],
      ["Have Sales approve seat-change replies and send them directly, and route every other reply through Patron Services' sign-off.", "Sales owns the booking record, not outgoing replies, so seat-change messages would bypass the team responsible for release."],
      ["Have Patron Services approve each draft at intake, then let the system insert the new seat and send it once Sales records the move.", "The completed reply containing the seat claim is never reviewed by Patron Services before it is sent."],
      ["Hold each outgoing reply until Sales clears its daily queue, then have Patron Services sign off the full batch together.", "Replies with no seat change are delayed by a dependency they do not have, which the workflow must avoid."]],
    ["theatre-config", "5.1", "projects", "Box office staff share a Project whose knowledge holds the current performance calendar. In separate chats, drafts follow the calendar but skip the required review note, 'Held for box office check,' on replies that mention seats. One supervisor has been adding the rule to her own chats' first message, and her drafts are the only correct ones. Where should the rule live?", "d", "Configure a Project by placing each requirement in the right layer: standing behavior in Project instructions, reference facts in knowledge, and run-specific details in the request. A rule that every chat needs belongs where every chat receives it, and should be tested in a fresh chat.",
      ["In the file name of the performance calendar in project knowledge, so Claude sees the rule whenever it reads the calendar.", "A file name is incidental metadata, not an instruction, and Claude may not consult the calendar for every seat reply."],
      ["In each staff member's account memory, by asking Claude to remember the rule once in a chat outside the Project.", "Memory is personal and inconsistent across staff, so the rule would apply only to some people and could not be maintained centrally."],
      ["In the supervisor's first message, copied into a shared document that staff paste at the start of each new chat.", "Manual pasting is the inconsistent practice that already fails; any staff member who forgets it reproduces the error."],
      ["In the Project instructions as a standing rule, with the calendar left in knowledge, then tested in a fresh Project chat.", "Project instructions apply to every chat in the Project, so the note appears consistently, and the fresh-chat test confirms it."]],
    ["theatre-connector", "5.2", "projects", "The pilot needs Claude to read public performance availability from a Google Drive folder. The same shared drive holds a restricted donor file and staff HR folders. The Drive connector authenticates as the user, and the pilot account can currently open the whole shared drive. Which connector configuration fits least privilege?", "b", "A connector reaches whatever the authenticated account can reach, so least privilege is set by account permissions, not by prompts. Narrow the account to what the task needs, then test that excluded content is unreachable before relying on the connection.",
      ["Connect the whole shared drive with the current account, and add a Project instruction telling Claude to open only the availability folder.", "An instruction limits what Claude is asked to do, not what the connector can reach; donor and HR files stay accessible."],
      ["Use a pilot account whose Drive access covers only the availability folder, connect it, and test that donor files are unreachable.", "Access is limited at the permission level and then verified, so restricted files cannot enter the conversation."],
      ["Connect the finance team's account, which already reaches the donor file, and ask Claude to retrieve only calendar items from it.", "This widens access to the most sensitive file and relies on a request rather than a permission boundary."],
      ["Upload the entire shared drive into project knowledge once, so Claude searches a static copy instead of live connected folders.", "Uploading everything discloses donor and HR files to the Project and leaves a snapshot that will go stale."]],
    ["theatre-purpose", "6.3", "privacy", "Marketing wants to upload six months of patron support chat logs to a Claude Project to find themes for a campaign. The logs were approved for incident investigation only, and they contain names and seat numbers. Marketing already has technical access to the export. The team lead says the data 'already lives in our systems.' What should happen first?", "b", "Approval is tied to purpose, not to where the data sits or who can technically reach it. A new purpose needs the data owner's approval, including which fields may be used, before any upload. Retention settings and in-tool redaction act after disclosure.",
      ["Upload only the most recent month of logs to reduce exposure, and analyze it in an incognito chat so nothing is remembered.", "Less data is still data used for an unapproved purpose, and incognito controls memory, not whether the upload is permitted."],
      ["Ask the data owner to approve the marketing purpose and permitted fields, then prepare just those fields before uploading.", "This obtains authorization for the new purpose and minimizes the data to what that approval allows."],
      ["Proceed under the existing incident approval, since the data comes from the same source and marketing already has access.", "The approval covers incident investigation; marketing use is a different purpose that the owner has not authorized."],
      ["Upload the full logs and ask Claude to redact names and seat numbers before it summarizes the campaign themes.", "Names and seat numbers are disclosed at upload, before any redaction, and the purpose itself remains unapproved."]],
    ["theatre-isolate", "7.1", "eval", "After switching to a new calendar feed, 6 of 40 notices describe preview performances as public shows. The old feed labelled them 'PREVIEW'; the new feed uses the status code 'P2'. The prompt, model and Project instructions have not changed. A colleague suggests switching to Opus to see whether it reads the feed better. What test isolates whether the input mapping is responsible?", "d", "Diagnose poor output by changing one variable at a time. When the only known change is an input format, replay the same record in both formats with prompt and model fixed. A model swap or template change adds variables and can mask the real cause.",
      ["Switch the Project to Opus and rerun the six notices, since a stronger model may work out that the code P2 means preview.", "A model swap changes a second variable, and a lucky inference would hide the unmapped code instead of fixing it."],
      ["Lengthen the notice template so each notice lists both preview and public performances, then check the next 40 notices.", "Changing the template adds another variable and does not test how the new status code is being interpreted."],
      ["Test sold-out public shows from the new feed, since those generate the most patron complaints when they are wrong.", "The failure affects previews, so testing public shows never exercises the path where the mislabeling occurs."],
      ["Replay one performance through the old and new feeds with the prompt and model held fixed, and see how each one is labelled.", "Only the input format differs, so a different label isolates the P2 mapping as the cause of the error."]],
    ["theatre-improve", "7.3", "eval", "Box office staff recheck every seat hold by hand once an hour, about 90 minutes a shift, because two drafts confirmed expired holds last season. The team proposes a Skill that runs the hold check with code execution before drafting and flags expired holds. Staff approval before sending must stay. Which pilot tests the better workflow safely?", "c", "Pilot a workflow change alongside the existing control before removing it, and measure both the error it targets and the effort it saves. Retiring a safeguard after a few passes, or measuring speed alone, can hide failures the old check was catching.",
      ["Retire the hourly manual check once the Skill passes its first ten test runs, and track patron complaints for the rest of the season.", "Ten runs is thin evidence, and complaints would reveal missed holds only after patrons were wrongly confirmed."],
      ["Track how fast Claude drafts confirmations with the Skill installed, and adopt it if drafting speed improves over two weeks.", "Drafting speed says nothing about whether expired holds are caught, which is the reason the manual check exists."],
      ["Run the Skill alongside the manual check for two weeks, counting missed expired holds and staff minutes, with sign-off kept.", "Running both shows whether the Skill catches what the manual check catches and how much time it saves, with no added risk."],
      ["Use one shared hold list for each performance and compare review times across the weeks when the schedule changes most.", "Schedule churn confounds the comparison, and a shared list does not test whether expired holds are detected."]],
  ]),
  ...caseQuestions("Apprenticeship intake", "A nonprofit opens a new apprenticeship cohort and handles intake in a shared Claude Project. Staff own awards and appeals. The office works from a versioned policy in project knowledge, redacted application extracts, applicant feedback and shared Project instructions. A pilot measures turnaround and errors for applicants and staff.", [
    ["intake-iterate", "1.3", "prompt", "Intake staff asked Claude to make rejection replies 'warmer and more encouraging.' In 12 of 30 revised replies, the paragraph explaining how to appeal, with its 10-day deadline, disappeared. The policy file and model are unchanged. A colleague proposes scoring the next batch by asking Claude how inclusive each reply sounds. How should the office evaluate a new prompt?", "b", "When a revision fixes one quality and breaks another, state what must stay invariant and evaluate each quality separately on the same cases. Keyword checks and model self-ratings are weak proxies that can pass a reply that has lost required content.",
      ["Add a warmer sample reply to the prompt and keep the version, old or new, that produces the shorter replies.", "Length is unrelated to the lost appeal paragraph, and a warmer sample does not make the appeal content mandatory."],
      ["Require the appeal paragraph and deadline, then score tone and appeal completeness as two criteria on the same 30 cases.", "The missing constraint is restated, and separate checks on matched cases show whether tone improved without losing content."],
      ["Accept a revised reply if it contains the word 'appeal,' since its presence shows that the appeal information was kept.", "A reply can mention appeal without the steps or the 10-day deadline, so the keyword check passes incomplete replies."],
      ["Ask Claude to rate how inclusive each reply sounds out of ten, and adopt the prompt whose replies score highest on average.", "A model rating its own tone is not independent evaluation, and it never checks whether appeal information survived."]],
    ["intake-types", "1.4", "prompt", "The program team wants two outputs: new interview questions that draw out applicants' practical experience, and a formal summary of what 64 applicants reported about barriers to attending. Only 41 answered the barriers question. An earlier combined prompt produced a summary listing brainstormed barriers no applicant mentioned. What instruction allows each task to succeed?", "d", "Match the prompting strategy to the task type. Ideation can range widely within constraints; a factual summary must stay inside its sources and state who is missing. Treating brainstorm output as evidence, or writing a report from general knowledge, blurs that boundary.",
      ["Use the brainstormed barriers as extra evidence in the summary, since they reflect issues the 23 non-respondents probably face.", "Brainstormed barriers are hypotheses, not reports; presenting them as findings attributes views to applicants who never gave them."],
      ["Ask for both outputs in one neutral report written from Claude's general knowledge of apprenticeships, so the style is consistent.", "General knowledge is not what these applicants reported, so the summary would not reflect the 41 actual responses."],
      ["Use a brainstorming prompt for both outputs so the interview questions and the summary both read as engaging and forward-looking.", "A brainstorming strategy invites invention, which is exactly what produced unsupported barriers in the earlier summary."],
      ["Brainstorm questions within stated constraints, then summarize only the 41 sourced responses and note that 23 did not answer.", "Each task gets its own strategy, and the summary is bounded by actual responses with the missing group stated."]],
    ["intake-slice", "2.2", "grounding", "A draft summary states, 'Evening workers abandon applications more often than other applicants.' The underlying export covers 180 full-time applicants and excludes all 75 part-time trainees, many of whom work evenings. The summary goes to the board next week. A colleague suggests softening 'more often' to 'somewhat more often.' What should go into the next draft?", "c", "Detect overgeneralization by comparing the claim's population with the data's population. Limit the claim to what was measured and name the excluded group. Softer adjectives, outside impressions and model-generated confidence scores do not supply evidence for people who were never in the data.",
      ["The original finding with 'somewhat' added, since the softer wording signals uncertainty without changing what the board takes away.", "Hedging the adjective leaves the claim covering part-time trainees, who are absent from the data entirely."],
      ["A general statement based on last year's staff impressions of evening workers, covering both full-time and part-time applicants.", "Staff impressions are not data about this cohort and cannot fill in the excluded part-time group."],
      ["The finding limited to the 180 full-time applicants, noting that the 75 part-time trainees were excluded and remain unmeasured.", "The claim now matches its evidence, and the board can see exactly which group still needs to be measured."],
      ["A confidence score Claude calculates from the feedback volume, with only the overall pattern shown so the board can compare rounds.", "A confidence score cannot compensate for a group missing from the data, and hiding detail makes the gap harder to see."]],
    ["intake-audience", "2.5", "prompt", "A verified pilot summary shows turnaround fell from 12 to 7 days, two scoring errors were caught in review, and the next intake closes on 30 June. The board must decide whether to extend the pilot; applicants need only the deadline and next step. A coordinator proposes sending both groups the full summary table for transparency. How should staff use the summary?", "a", "Adapt one verified fact set to each audience's decision or action. Decision makers need results, risks and the ask; the public needs the action and date. Identical content serves neither well, and separate sources for each version invite facts to drift apart.",
      ["Give the board a decision brief with results, errors caught and the extension ask; give applicants a plain deadline note.", "Each audience receives the verified facts it needs for its own decision or next step, drawn from the same summary."],
      ["Send both groups the same summary table with light rewording, so neither audience can claim that information was withheld.", "Applicants must dig through pilot metrics to find their deadline, and the board gets no clear decision request."],
      ["Ask Claude to write each version from different source files, so each reads as an original document for its audience.", "Different sources can produce different facts, such as conflicting dates, which defeats the point of one verified summary."],
      ["Send applicants the board's full internal analysis verbatim, since the pilot affects them and they may want the detail.", "Internal analysis is not written for applicants and buries the one action they need: applying by 30 June."]],
    ["intake-proof", "2.1", "grounding", "The coordinator has 20 test intake replies, including rejections with and without appeal rights. Policy says only applicants rejected on eligibility grounds may appeal, within 10 days. All drafts cite the current policy, and 17 include a deadline. Staff will spot-check replies later. Which acceptance criterion detects both missing deadlines and deadlines offered to the wrong applicants?", "d", "Fitness checks must test whether each element applies to the specific case before testing its presence or value. Counting presence rewards wrongly supplied content, and checking only supplied values makes omissions invisible. Case-level criteria derived from policy catch both failure types.",
      ["Measure the share of rejections that include a deadline and a current policy link, and look for improvement over the manual baseline.", "A higher share rewards deadlines given to applicants who cannot appeal, which is one of the two errors to catch."],
      ["Check that dates in replies containing deadline text are correct, and ask a second reviewer to spot-check the most ambiguous decisions.", "Checking only replies that mention a deadline makes an omitted but required deadline invisible to the main check."],
      ["Score submission instructions and citation links separately, and look into the appeal content only when applicants ask follow-up questions.", "Waiting for applicant questions finds errors after they cause harm and never tests eligibility for appeal."],
      ["For each case, check appeal eligibility against policy, then whether a deadline is required, present and correct for that applicant.", "Testing applicability first catches deadlines given wrongly, and testing presence and value catches omitted or wrong dates."]],
    ["intake-tier", "3.3", "models", "A 300-form trial found Haiku at default effort tags complete forms with 97% accuracy, meeting the target, but accuracy on mixed-format forms with handwritten pages drops to 71% and each error costs 15 minutes of rework. Mixed forms are 20% of volume. Sonnet at high effort reached 94% on mixed forms in a 30-form test. Which plan preserves the evidence-based trade-off?", "b", "Balance cost, speed and quality by routing each slice to the cheapest setup that meets its measured target, and treat small tests as provisional. Moving everything to a stronger model wastes cost where the cheap one works; keeping the cheap one everywhere ignores measured rework.",
      ["Move each form to Sonnet at high effort, since the mixed-form failures show Haiku is not reliable enough for intake work.", "Haiku already meets the target on complete forms, which are 80 percent of volume, so this adds cost without a quality gain."],
      ["Keep Haiku for complete forms, route mixed-format forms to Sonnet at high effort, and track rework on a larger mixed sample.", "Each slice uses the setup its evidence supports, and the larger sample tests whether the 30-form result holds."],
      ["Keep Haiku across intake and instruct it to guess missing fields on mixed forms, so turnaround stays predictable for staff.", "Guessing fields converts rework into silent errors on intake records, which is worse than visible corrections."],
      ["Keep Haiku for each form and add staff review of a regular random sample, keeping one model so processing time stays predictable.", "Random sampling spreads review across complete forms that are fine and misses most of the mixed forms that fail."]],
    ["intake-fresh", "3.4", "claudeMemory", "Two closed intake cases belong to applicants named J. Rivera. The coordinator's long chat and her account memory both mention each applicant's interview date. A colleague now needs a reply for case A-214 only, drawn from the approved appointment record. In a test, a fresh chat suggested case A-209's date. What reduces carryover for this reply?", "a", "A fresh chat still draws on memory, so carryover from similar past work can persist. For a task where prior context could mislead, turn memory off for that chat before the first message and supply the exact identifiers and source the task needs.",
      ["Open a fresh chat, turn memory off from the '+' menu before the first message, and paste case A-214's ID and interview date.", "Turning memory off for this chat removes the remembered dates, and explicit case details anchor the reply to the right record."],
      ["Open a fresh chat with memory on, refer to the applicant by name alone, and let Claude recall which interview date applies.", "Memory holds both applicants' dates, and the shared name is exactly what caused the wrong date in the test."],
      ["Continue the existing chat and paste both interview dates without case IDs, asking Claude to pick the one for this reply.", "The long chat contains both cases, and supplying both dates without IDs invites the same confusion."],
      ["Add both applicants' interview dates to the Project instructions so later chats can see and compare the two cases.", "Case facts in standing instructions spread closed-case details into every chat and make carryover more likely."]],
    ["intake-wait", "4.2", "eval", "Applications take a median of 16 days to decide. Logs show reviewers spend about 20 minutes on a complete form, but 35% of forms arrive missing a signature and wait a median of 9 days while staff email the applicant. The director proposes a larger model to write more detailed decline letters. Where is the most useful pilot intervention?", "c", "Research a process by locating where time is lost, then intervene at that point and measure the affected slice separately. A change aimed at a stage that is not the bottleneck, or a measure that excludes the problem cases, cannot show improvement.",
      ["Use a more capable model for decline letters so applicants understand decisions better, then measure complaint volume next cohort.", "Decline letters are not where the 9-day wait occurs, so this leaves the main delay untouched."],
      ["Give reviewers extra time to check signatures after each application arrives, then report how long complete forms spend at that desk.", "Checking later in the process keeps the email loop that causes the delay, and the measure excludes incomplete forms."],
      ["Check signatures at submission and prompt applicants at once, then measure waiting time for complete and incomplete forms apart.", "Catching missing signatures at intake targets the 9-day loop, and the split measure shows whether it shortens."],
      ["Measure decision time only for forms that already had signatures, since those cases show the review process at its best.", "Excluding unsigned forms removes the cases that drive the delay, so the pilot could not reveal any improvement."]],
    ["intake-proof-form", "4.3", "eval", "Staff want to cut omitted references in award recommendations, currently 14 per 100. They are considering two changes: a revised intake form that requires reference contacts, and a new drafting prompt that lists required sections. They have 200 matched past cases and two weeks. How can they learn which intervention reduced omissions?", "a", "When designing and revising a solution with more than one candidate change, test each change alone and together on matched cases against a baseline. That shows which change helped and whether they interact; bundled launches, cherry-picked cases and self-estimates cannot attribute the effect.",
      ["Run baseline, form-only, prompt-only and combined versions on the same matched cases, and compare omission rates for each.", "Each change is isolated on identical inputs, so the team sees each effect and any interaction between the two."],
      ["Launch both changes together for the next cohort and compare its omission rate with last year's completed cohort.", "Two simultaneous changes and a different cohort leave the source of any improvement impossible to identify."],
      ["Test the combined version on the cleanest 50 submissions first, since messy cases would obscure the effect of the changes.", "Clean cases are where omissions are rarest, so the test may show no difference and still not isolate either change."],
      ["Ask the prompt author and form designer to estimate their change's contribution, and weight future effort by those estimates.", "Self-estimates from each change's author are opinions, not measurements, and are likely to favor their own work."]],
    ["intake-board", "4.5", "eval", "The pilot saved 10 minutes on complete forms and added 6 minutes on ambiguous forms. The pilot sample was 90% complete forms, but historically 40% of applications are ambiguous. The board chair asks for 'the time saving per form' to quote in a funding bid. What should the board hear?", "b", "Explain value with the limits of the evidence attached. When the sample mix differs from real workload, a single average misleads; report results by slice, name the imbalance, and say how it will be closed before a headline figure is quoted externally.",
      ["A ten-minute saving per form, since complete forms made up nine in ten pilot cases and represent the normal workload.", "Historically 40 percent of forms are ambiguous and those cost time, so this overstates the saving considerably."],
      ["Results by form type, a note that the sample under-represents ambiguous forms, and a plan to study them before quoting a figure.", "The board sees what was measured, why the blended figure would mislead, and how a defensible number will be obtained."],
      ["An average saving of 8.4 minutes per form across the pilot sample, with the number of cases shown and further analysis deferred.", "The average reflects the skewed sample; with a realistic mix the saving is far smaller, so quoting it misleads funders."],
      ["A productivity gain based on Claude's drafting speed, which is the part of the workflow the pilot was designed to change.", "Drafting speed ignores the extra time ambiguous forms took, so it overstates the benefit to the whole process."]],
    ["intake-rule", "5.3", "projects", "Six coordinators draft intake replies in a shared Project. Every reply must send unresolved eligibility questions to staff instead of answering them, but an audit found 5 of 40 replies offering eligibility opinions, all from coordinators who forgot to add the rule to their prompt. The rule never changes. What is the most maintainable approach?", "c", "Rules that every interaction needs belong in persistent instructions so they do not depend on each person's memory. Dated reference files, saved chats and meeting reminders are fragile carriers for standing behavior. Confirm the rule with a fresh chat on the edge case it targets.",
      ["Ask each coordinator to type the referral rule into each prompt, and add a reminder to the team's weekly meeting agenda.", "This keeps the manual step that already failed in 5 of 40 replies; a reminder does not stop people forgetting."],
      ["Add the referral rule to this month's dated policy bulletin in project knowledge, so Claude reads it when consulting the bulletin.", "A dated bulletin will be replaced, and knowledge is consulted for facts, so the rule may be missed or retired with the file."],
      ["Add the referral rule to the Project instructions, then try it in a fresh chat with an ambiguous eligibility question.", "Project instructions apply to every chat, so the rule no longer depends on each coordinator, and the test confirms it works."],
      ["Save the best resolved conversation as a template chat and ask coordinators to continue new cases inside that thread.", "Continuing one thread mixes applicants' cases in shared context and does not make the rule a standing instruction."]],
    ["intake-sensitive", "6.2", "privacy", "The pilot may send descriptions of paperwork problems to Claude, but policy bars learner IDs and case notes containing medical details. An analyst prepares a 600-row export for code-executed analysis of problem types. The steps are: 1) Upload the extract to the chat. 2) Classify each field against the policy. 3) Screen free-text descriptions for names and diagnoses. 4) Remove learner IDs and medical case notes locally. 5) Run the analysis. Which order fits the policy?", "a", "Protect data in order: classify what policy allows, remove excluded fields, screen the free text that remains, and only then upload. Free text is the common place where identifiers survive. Any order that uploads before screening has already disclosed whatever the screen would have caught.",
      ["2, 4, 3, 1, 5", "Fields are classified, excluded columns removed and free text screened, all before the upload, so only permitted content leaves."],
      ["2, 1, 4, 3, 5", "The extract is uploaded before IDs and medical notes are removed, so barred data has already reached Claude."],
      ["4, 2, 3, 1, 5", "Removing fields before classifying them means the analyst is guessing which columns the policy actually bars."],
      ["2, 4, 1, 3, 5", "Free text is screened after upload, so any names or diagnoses in descriptions have already been disclosed."]],
    ["intake-label", "6.4", "eval", "An intake classifier labels 22% of short messages 'low motivation' but only 4% of long messages. In a check of 30 pairs with identical facts and requested accommodations, the short version got the label 11 times and the long version twice. Staff use the label to order interviews. What should the team do?", "d", "When matched evidence shows outcomes changing with writing style rather than facts, remove the unsupported judgment from the decision path and route on relevant facts. Then rerun the matched test to confirm the disparity closes. Hiding the label or excluding cases leaves the effect in place.",
      ["Keep the label but use it internally as a secondary priority signal, so applicants do not see how they were described.", "Hidden labels still change who is interviewed first, so applicants who write briefly are still disadvantaged."],
      ["Exclude short messages from the quality report so that the classifier's accuracy is measured on comparable, information-rich cases only.", "Excluding the affected group hides the disparity from reporting while it continues to shape interview order."],
      ["Check overall agreement between the classifier and staff across the full applicant pool, and keep the label if agreement exceeds 85%.", "High overall agreement can coexist with systematic bias against short messages, which the matched pairs already show."],
      ["Drop the motivation label, order interviews on stated facts and needs, and rerun the short and long pairs to see the gap close.", "The unsupported inference leaves the decision path, and the matched rerun verifies that writing length no longer drives order."]],
    ["intake-eligibility", "6.1", "safety", "A vendor offers a plugin that bundles a scoring Skill and a connector to the applicant database, and proposes that Claude issue award decisions with reasons. Program rules require a staff decision with documented reasons, and the Usage Policy treats education and employment decisions about people as high-risk. Which pilot use fits?", "b", "Judge both the use and the extension. High-risk decisions about people need a qualified human who decides with authority to change the recommendation, and a third-party plugin's bundled Skills and connectors need review of what they access before installation. Sampling, tie-breaking and misattributed reasons fail one or both tests.",
      ["Install the plugin and let it grant awards for routine cases automatically, with staff reviewing a weekly sample of its decisions.", "The plugin is installed without review, and most award decisions would be made without a person deciding each case."],
      ["After IT vets what the plugin can access, Claude prepares cited summaries against the criteria and staff decide each award.", "Staff keep the documented decision, and the plugin's database access is reviewed before it touches applicant records."],
      ["Use the plugin's generated reasons to approve the applicants it rates most motivated, and tell applicants the reasons came from staff.", "The model decides, and misattributing its reasons to staff breaks the transparency the Usage Policy requires."],
      ["Use Claude's output to settle cases where two staff reviewers disagree, logging those cases for the policy team's next audit.", "The hardest cases would be decided by the model, which is the opposite of keeping a qualified human in the decision."]],
    ["intake-review", "7.2", "eval", "Eight of ten reviewers flagged replies quoting a 15 May appeal deadline that the current policy changed to 22 May; one flagged tone. The obsolete date came from a 'deadlines' note saved in Project memory last term. Project knowledge holds only the current policy. What should go into the next release test?", "c", "Use reviewer feedback to find the shared root cause, fix it at its source, and add the failing cases as a regression test. Style changes, length limits and setting changes address symptoms or other issues and leave the stale memory free to reappear.",
      ["A tone comparison between two available models on the same replies, since one reviewer judged the current tone too formal.", "One tone flag matters less than eight wrong deadlines, and changing models leaves the stale memory note in place."],
      ["A check that every reply is under 100 words, since shorter replies leave less room for outdated deadlines to appear.", "A short reply can still quote 15 May; length does not remove the stale memory the date comes from."],
      ["Delete the stale memory entry, then replay the replies that quoted 15 May and confirm they now cite 22 May from policy.", "Removing the source of the error and retesting the exact failing cases confirms the root cause is fixed."],
      ["Switch to a higher effort setting with the same Project setup, so Claude weighs the policy file more carefully against memory.", "More effort does not delete the stale note, and the model may still prefer remembered context in some replies."]],
  ]),
];
